@benmccann would you be able to review this PR and check if most use cases discussed in https://github.com/sveltejs/kit/issues/595 (or involve others from the Svelte community that may be interested in helping push this change forward?)

@wighawag it would be good if you could check this PR, and see if it would help you with https://github.com/wighawag/sveltejs-adapter-ipfs

For context, this PR may be later complemented with an option to define the URL for the assets path dynamically (and public files path, but I think SvelteKit isn't using the public folder), https://github.com/vitejs/vite/issues/3522#issuecomment-1090327274 (still keeping relative as many of the URLs as possible)

I've created a simple demo to illustrate what I mean in https://github.com/vitejs/vite/pull/7644#issuecomment-1126198509: https://github.com/Rich-Harris/vite-relative-base-repro.

The tl;dr is that as far as I can tell, this PR doesn't solve the problem unfortunately. Dependencies need to be resolved against the importing module, _not_ the document.

In the case where `entryFileNames`, `chunkFileNames` and `assetFileNames` are all siblings, this ought to be straightforward since `__vitePreload` can resolve against its own `import.meta.url`. I imagine it's slightly more complex in a situation like this:

```js
rollupOptions: {
  input: {...},
  output: {
    format: 'esm',
    entryFileNames: '[name].js',
    chunkFileNames: 'chunks/[name]-[hash].js',
    assetFileNames: 'assets/[name]-[hash][extname]'
  },
  // ...
}
```

Ah, nice — as of https://github.com/vitejs/vite/pull/7644/commits/9681acabdd75dda2fcd8798ee2becc57b63544ab, it works as expected as long as entries, chunks and assets are siblings...

```diff
rollupOptions: {
  input: {...},
  output: {
    format: 'esm',
    entryFileNames: '[name].js',
-    chunkFileNames: 'chunks/[name]-[hash].js',
-    assetFileNames: 'assets/[name]-[hash][extname]'
+    chunkFileNames: '[name]-[hash].js',
+    assetFileNames: '[name]-[hash][extname]'
  },
  // ...
}
```

...and `[name]` doesn't include a `/` character. This is huge! If it's not possible to relax those constraints (which I would totally understand) I wonder if it's worth throwing an error if `base` is relative and `input.*` or `output.[entry|chunk|asset]FileNames` contains a `/`?

